
Difference Between Hashtable and ConcurrentHashMap

Hashtable vs ConcurrentHashMap (Point Wise)
1. Thread Safety
Hashtable → ✅ Thread Safe
ConcurrentHashMap → ✅ Thread Safe

2. Performance
Hashtable → Slow
Entire map is locked for every operation.
ConcurrentHashMap → Fast
Only bucket/portion of map is locked.

3. Locking Mechanism

Hashtable

Uses method-level synchronization.
Entire map gets locked.

ConcurrentHashMap

Uses bucket-level locking + CAS (Compare And Swap).
Only affected bucket gets locked.

4. Multiple Threads Access
Hashtable
Only one thread can access the map at a time.
Thread-1 -> Running
2
Thread-2 -> Waiting
3
Thread-3 -> Waiting

ConcurrentHashMap
Multiple threads can work simultaneously on different buckets.
Thread-1 -> Bucket-1
2
Thread-2 -> Bucket-2
3
Thread-3 -> Bucket-3

Both Hashtable and ConcurrentHashMap are thread-safe. Hashtable synchronizes the entire map, allowing only one thread to access it at a time, which makes it slower. ConcurrentHashMap provides thread safety using bucket-level locking and CAS operations, allowing multiple threads to read and write concurrently. Therefore, ConcurrentHashMap offers much better performance and is the preferred choice in modern Java and Spring Boot applications.
