IdentityHashMap in Java
======================

IdentityHashMap is a special implementation of the Map interface that compares keys using reference equality (==) instead of object equality (equals()). It also uses System.identityHashCode() instead of hashCode()

How it Works
HashMap
String s1 = new String("Java");
String s2 = new String("Java");

Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put(s1, 100);

System.out.println(map.get(s2)); // 100
Works because s1.equals(s2) is true.

HashMap uses equals() and hashCode() to compare keys, whereas IdentityHashMap uses reference equality (==) and System.identityHashCode(). If two objects contain the same data but are different instances, HashMap treats them as the same key, while IdentityHashMap treats them as different keys. IdentityHashMap is mainly used for object identity tracking, serialization frameworks, and scenarios where object references matter more than object content.
